      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "To my knowledge, and based on my limited readings of the API protocols used for avatars, the reason it will occasionally take multiple clicks is because changing avatars is rate-limited, and each user in an instance is counted towards that limit.\n\nThe limit, to my knowledge, is only 1 to 5 seconds, depending on avatar performance and server load, though far more on avatar performance. If someone else tries to load an avatar rated very poor, it can take 5 seconds, and if it is rated to be better on data cost, it can take less, though no less than a single second. Each time someone changes an avatar, VRC has to send that data to everyone in the instance, and that takes time and data, and data transfers cost them money.\n\nI have experienced this in public worlds on USE and USW, and had absolutely no issue with it at all in my home instance unless I was loading a very poor avatar, realized within under a second that I had picked the wrong avatar, and made it back to the avatar menu to change avatars a second time before that five second limit had been reached.\n\nLong story short, the issue isn't that avatars aren't changing fast enough, but rather that they don't have any indicator to show people when a rate limit is being imposed, and that they don't have a priority queue system to load the avatar once the limit ends in order of who tried to change their avatar first.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
